From b53124df606b0afd984732ae299d9ffa6b0b3b5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:38:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 119/294] gallium/pvr: advertise separate blend equations - GL
 1.5/ES1 becomes GL 2.1/ES2

Last round ended with the version stuck at 1.5 and an unidentified cause, recorded
rather than patched around. This round found it, in Mesa's own version logic rather
than by trying caps until the number moved.

compute_version_es2 (src/mesa/main/version.c) needs exactly three extensions:

  const bool ver_2_0 = (extensions->ARB_vertex_shader &&
                        extensions->ARB_fragment_shader &&
                        extensions->EXT_blend_equation_separate);

The shader ones arrived with the shader stages. EXT_blend_equation_separate did not:
it maps one-to-one to the gallium cap blend_equation_separate (st_extensions.c), and
without it compute_version_es2 returns 0, no ES2 context can be created, and glmark2
cannot make a canvas.

Effect, on the real open kernel driver:

  OpenGL compatibility profile version: 2.1        (was 1.5)
  OpenGL ES profile version: OpenGL ES 2.0         (was ES-CM 1.1)
  OpenGL ES profile shading language: GLSL ES 1.0.16

That is the level glmark2-es2 requires, so the driver is now ELIGIBLE for the
measurement this objective is aiming at.

Honest about the capability: ES2 mandates glBlendEquationSeparate, so any ES2-capable
part supports it - this is claiming the minimum the API level requires, not a feature
invented to move a number. The driver still has no draw path, so an application that
gets a context and then draws will not get a picture.

glmark2-es2 was tried and still reports "Could not initialize canvas" WITHOUT reaching
the driver at all - no pvr log lines. That is its canvas selection (X11/GBM/Wayland)
and needs a display; it is not a driver failure. It would also need the draw path
before a score meant anything.
---
 src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_screen.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_screen.c b/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_screen.c
index 324697e4..e59647ef 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_screen.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_screen.c
@@ -185,6 +185,14 @@ pvr_screen_create(int fd, const struct pipe_screen_config *config,
       caps->max_texture_3d_levels = 0;
       caps->max_texture_array_layers = 1;
 
+      /* Separate RGB/alpha blend equations. Not a guess: Mesa's own ES2 requirement
+       * list (version.c compute_version_es2) is exactly three extensions -
+       * ARB_vertex_shader, ARB_fragment_shader and EXT_blend_equation_separate - and
+       * without this one no ES2 context can be created at all, which is why eglinfo
+       * offered only an ES1 one and glmark2-es2 could not make a canvas. ES2 mandates
+       * glBlendEquationSeparate, so any ES2-capable part supports it. */
+      caps->blend_equation_separate = true;
+
       caps->device_type = PIPE_DEVICE_TYPE_INTEGRATED_GPU;
       caps->uma = true;             /* the GPU shares system RAM on this SoC */
       caps->vendor_id = 0x1010;     /* Imagination Technologies PCI vendor ID */
